WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Can an enzyme make an impossible reaction happen?

2
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.460
Or can it only make a possible one fast?

3
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.700
Start with a reaction your cells need to make RNA.

4
00:00:14.317 --> 00:00:16.777
On its own, this step is painfully slow.

5
00:00:18.197 --> 00:00:21.497
Half the molecules would take 78 million years to react.

6
00:00:22.777 --> 00:00:25.337
With its enzyme, the step is done in milliseconds.

7
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.600
That is a speed-up of about 10¹⁷.

8
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:33.660
to the 17. So could an enzyme that strong push a reaction uphill?

9
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.920
No. To see why, split one question into two.

10
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:43.633
Which valley is lower decides which way the reaction runs.

11
00:00:44.793 --> 00:00:46.913
That is thermodynamics, delta ΔG.

12
00:00:48.153 --> 00:00:50.213
How high the ridge is decides how fast.

13
00:00:51.433 --> 00:00:52.433
That is kinetics.

14
00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:57.547
That climb is the activation energy, delta G double dagger.

15
00:00:58.727 --> 00:01:01.087
At the very top sits the transition state.

16
00:01:02.347 --> 00:01:06.387
Its bonds are partly breaking and forming, and it lasts femtoseconds.

17
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:08.750
Here is the whole idea.

18
00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:11.570
An enzyme lowers the ridge.

19
00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.150
It never moves the valleys.

20
00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:20.050
It opens a lower pass over the same ridge, still a highest point, just lower.

21
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.250
A little lower buys a lot of speed.

22
00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:31.110
At 25 °C, every 5.7 kJ/mol taken off makes it ten times faster.

23
00:01:32.183 --> 00:01:35.363
Take off about 97, and you reach that 10¹⁷.

24
00:01:35.363 --> 00:01:40.163
to the 17. Now flip the trip, from product back to substrate.

25
00:01:41.317 --> 00:01:44.457
Say the enzyme speeds the forward trip a millionfold.

26
00:01:45.777 --> 00:01:48.177
Watch both arrows cross the same lowered top.

27
00:01:49.397 --> 00:01:50.977
What happens to the backward trip?

28
00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:57.830
Also a millionfold.

29
00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.970
Counted from the two valleys, both trips cross the same lowered top.

30
00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:07.610
So their ratio, the equilibrium constant, stays the same.

31
00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.970
The enzyme reaches the same equilibrium, only sooner.

32
00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:14.930
It cannot push a reaction uphill.

33
00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:19.910
If the far valley is higher, the net reaction runs the other way.

34
00:02:20.933 --> 00:02:22.953
So how does a protein lower the top?

35
00:02:24.213 --> 00:02:28.273
Its active site better matches the transition state than the substrate.

36
00:02:29.493 --> 00:02:33.813
It holds the substrate more loosely and the transition state more snugly.

37
00:02:34.817 --> 00:02:37.897
That extra grip pays back energy right at the top.

38
00:02:39.097 --> 00:02:41.277
The climb from the bound substrate shrinks.

39
00:02:42.517 --> 00:02:47.377
That bound-substrate speed-up equals how many times tighter it grips the transition

40
00:02:47.377 --> 00:02:48.377
state.

41
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.980
An active site that gripped the substrate just as hard would gain nothing.

42
00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.220
Both would sink together, and the climb would stay the same.

43
00:02:58.283 --> 00:03:01.143
So the valleys decide which way a reaction runs.

44
00:03:02.383 --> 00:03:03.883
The ridge decides how fast.

45
00:03:05.123 --> 00:03:08.743
An enzyme touches only the ridge, by gripping the top hardest.
